+# ADR-0017: Consents are append-only records and publish `identity.consent-changed` with IDs only
+
+- **Status:** Proposed
+- **Date:** 2026-11-04
+- **Deciders:** <tech lead>, Sunny Bai
+- **Sources:** T3-AC-004, T3-D-009, [architecture.md section 4](../architecture.md#4-modules), [event conventions](../conventions/events.md), Jira AN-900
+
+## Context
+
+T3-AC-004 asks that a consent record shows its type, its text version, its date, its source and its withdrawal, so that head office can prove each consent. The `identity` module owns consents (architecture section 4), and the architecture lists "consent changed" as one of its events. Other modules (journey messages, later the e-mail marketing tool through `integration`) must learn about each change, but must not read the `identity` tables. The event conventions say a payload holds IDs, not copies of personal data. The consent decision itself (given or withdrawn) is personal data.
+
+## Options
+
+1. **Append-only records, event with IDs only.** `identity.consent-changed` carries `consentId`, `personId`, `purpose`; a reacting module reads the decision with `identity.currentConsent`. Good: no personal data on the queue or in the outbox; one source of the decision; a late or repeated event still reads the latest state, so handlers stay idempotent. Bad: a reacting module makes one read per event.
+2. **Append-only records, event with the full decision.** The payload also carries `granted`, `channel`, `textVersion`, `decidedAt`. Good: no read. Bad: personal data copied into the queue and every consumer, against the event conventions; an out-of-order delivery can apply an old decision.
+3. **One mutable row per person and purpose, with an audit log.** Good: a simple current read. Bad: the proof lives in a second structure; a change of the row loses the proof unless the log is complete, against "records are never changed or deleted".
+
+## Decision
+
+We choose option 1. It keeps personal data out of the queue, keeps the proof in one append-only table, and makes handlers idempotent by reading the current state. The purpose is in the payload so that a consumer can ignore purposes it does not handle without a read.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Head office proves a consent from `identity.consent` alone: every decision is a row with its text version, channel and time.
+- The repository port (`ConsentRepository`) has only `append` and `listByPerson`; there is no update or delete. The Drizzle repository must keep that shape, and the database role of the application should have no `UPDATE` or `DELETE` grant on `identity.consent` (to be set with the migration).
+- A consumer reads `currentConsent` after each event. The cost is one indexed read (`person_id, purpose, decided_at`).
+- Tests: `packages/core/src/identity/consent.test.ts` (T3-AC-004 and the event payload); `pnpm docs` checks that the event is listed in the module file.
